And I include not only my personhood, my personality, but the whole thing, the body as well, you see.

24
00:02:36,832 --> 00:02:39,735
And the word personality, of course, is quite primped.

25
00:02:39,836 --> 00:02:48,884
It's an Oxford dictionary says it's a combination of characteristics and qualities that form an individual's distinctive character.

26
00:02:49,625 --> 00:02:54,370
So he has an engaging personality, although he is a liar and a thief.

27
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:58,033
I made that one up myself.

28
00:02:58,054 --> 00:03:05,540
So we'll just go very quickly through what we did yesterday so that you can see the connection.

29
00:03:05,842 --> 00:03:10,890
But I will start with Dan Medina's victory verse.

30
00:03:10,911 --> 00:03:18,864
So there's a whole collection of Te Rigata, which are the verses that were left by the various nuns who became fully liberated.

31
00:03:19,765 --> 00:03:21,588
And this was her verse.

32
00:03:22,042 --> 00:03:27,556
One who is eager and determined would be filled with awareness, right?

33
00:03:27,575 --> 00:03:36,759
So if you're really eager, if we are really eager and determined to become liberated from suffering, then we'll really work on that sense of awareness.

34
00:03:37,752 --> 00:03:43,580
One whose mind is not tied up with sensual pleasure is said to be heading upstream.

35
00:03:44,241 --> 00:04:07,252
Now, in the myths that surrounds the Buddha's own process, remember, he put the bowl in the river and it went upstream, which means that everything we're doing spiritually is really against the flow of normal society, which is basically about getting excited, becoming a consumer and enjoying the five sensual chords, as they're called.

36
00:04:07,856 --> 00:04:09,318
So that was her verse.

37
00:04:09,538 --> 00:04:13,224
One who is eager and determined would be filled with awareness.

38
00:04:13,925 --> 00:04:19,233
One whose mind is not tied up with sensual pleasure is said to be heading upstream.

39
00:04:20,475 --> 00:04:22,678
So that was obviously her path.

40
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:23,639
That's what she's telling us.

41
00:04:24,161 --> 00:04:31,992
Made a great effort to be constantly aware and not to get caught up in indulging the sensual pleasures.

42
00:04:34,283 --> 00:04:37,346
Remember, she came from a rich family there.

43
00:04:39,189 --> 00:04:53,827
Visakha had been, I think, yes, he was a trader, a businessman, and he had listened to the Buddha's words and become a non-returner, which is pretty high up.

44
00:04:54,144 --> 00:04:57,716
the steps of spiritual practice.

45
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,154
And then he wasn't interested in being a householder anymore.

46
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:05,944
He slept in a different room, which upset Kisarga.

47
00:05:06,396 --> 00:05:08,478
And finally upset Damodina.

48
00:05:08,978 --> 00:05:12,581
And finally Damodina thought, well, I may as well become a nun.

49
00:05:12,882 --> 00:05:14,384
So she went to become a nun.

50
00:05:14,884 --> 00:05:17,266
And she, of course, attained full liberation.

51
00:05:17,786 --> 00:05:20,910
So here we have a very strange situation for those times.

52
00:05:21,389 --> 00:05:25,973
Here is a husband who is asking his wife to guide him.

53
00:05:25,994 --> 00:05:28,315
I mean, that's unheard of in those times, remember?

54
00:05:28,615 --> 00:05:29,978
Yeah.

55
00:05:30,098 --> 00:05:36,083
And of course, she is named by the Buddha as the foremost in preaching or teaching.

56
00:05:37,091 --> 00:05:43,819
So the first question was identity or personhood, personhood.

57
00:05:44,238 --> 00:05:45,740
What does the Buddha call that?

58
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:46,762
You see the blessed one.

59
00:05:46,822 --> 00:05:48,322
He said, well, it's clinging.

60
00:05:48,423 --> 00:05:59,293
It is this identity with the five aggregates, our bodies, our feelings, perceptions, concepts, all our habits and consciousness.

61
00:06:01,896 --> 00:06:03,858
And then he asked the question,

62
00:06:05,915 --> 00:06:07,057
What's the origin of this?

63
00:06:07,076 --> 00:06:08,459
What's the cause?

64
00:06:08,959 --> 00:06:11,161
Again, he's put the origin, but it's the cause of it.

65
00:06:11,483 --> 00:06:19,153
The cause is this tanha, this wrong desire of seeking happiness, real happiness in the sensual world.

66
00:06:20,454 --> 00:06:24,560
And the cessation of this personhood or self.

67
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:27,184
See, that's another word we could have used, self.

68
00:06:27,625 --> 00:06:30,588
But sakaya is

69
00:06:30,956 --> 00:06:34,928
Kaya is the psychological self.

70
00:06:35,028 --> 00:06:37,617
It's a way of referring to it, but it also includes the body.

71
00:06:37,697 --> 00:06:42,672
So it has a bigger sort of meaning, you might say, and it's actually used

72
00:06:43,005 --> 00:06:48,531
When somebody has that first insight into stream entry, that's the view that goes.

73
00:06:48,591 --> 00:06:55,677
It's the view of self, that view of being a self that disappears, but not the sense of self.

74
00:06:55,697 --> 00:06:58,740
Still, that feeling remains of being this human being.

75
00:06:59,199 --> 00:07:01,781
And that doesn't go until we're fully liberated.

76
00:07:04,064 --> 00:07:05,605
So what is the sense?

77
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:11,511
Well, it's the remainderless fading away, giving up, relinquishing, letting go and rejecting that same craving.

78
00:07:13,617 --> 00:07:28,781
Now, remember, there are 16 personality views, 20 personality views based on the five aggregates and the four ways of relating to them.

79
00:07:29,201 --> 00:07:30,985
So the first one was

80
00:07:31,218 --> 00:07:44,838
course uh to regard the body as self feelings of self etc etc so that was a pure identity the next one was to sort of possess it i have a bad knee and so forth

81
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:49,593
The next one is to feel bigger than the body.

82
00:07:50,475 --> 00:07:53,798
The self feels bigger than the psychophysical organism.

83
00:07:54,238 --> 00:08:04,629
As when you feel really fit, you feel good, you feel happy and you take your body for a walk and you feel that, you know, that sense of me is bigger than this body.

84
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:13,779
On the other hand, the opposite is true when you feel possessed or trapped in the body, especially when it's ill or not feeling well.

85
00:08:15,785 --> 00:08:19,218
So those are your four different types of identity.

86
00:08:19,579 --> 00:08:24,478
And when you multiply that by the five aggregates, then you have 20 types of identity.

87
00:08:25,757 --> 00:08:36,653
And of course, the question arises from that, how does this personhood view or self view or identity view not come to be?

88
00:08:36,774 --> 00:08:37,534
How does it stop?

89
00:08:37,634 --> 00:08:51,315
Well, it's by perceiving the delusive nature of that and perceiving that there is that these five, these four modes of identity are just notions in the mind.

90
00:08:51,375 --> 00:08:53,638
They're just they're just something that we make up.

91
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:08,365
That comes from, if we go back a step, that comes from the delusion that lies within the awareness itself, the intuitive awareness.

92
00:09:09,407 --> 00:09:15,120
Remember, it finds itself within these five khandhas and presumes itself to be that.

93
00:09:15,673 --> 00:09:26,825
And in our meditation, when we pull back within ourselves to find that observation post, and we're looking at these five candles, these five heaps, right?

94
00:09:26,845 --> 00:09:36,995
We're looking at the cycle, physical organism, that separation is telling us something about the relationship of awareness with this organism, right?

95
00:09:37,014 --> 00:09:41,479
It's beginning to pot is beginning to feel that it's, it, it, it, it,

96
00:09:41,812 --> 00:09:43,559
It doesn't have that identity.

97
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:46,673
The identity begins to be undermined.

98
00:09:46,692 --> 00:09:49,725
Okay?

99
00:09:49,745 --> 00:09:52,296
I mean, that's the core purpose of Adipasana.

100
00:09:54,638 --> 00:09:58,100
And then we went on to the Eightfold Path, which is a simple question.

101
00:09:58,140 --> 00:09:59,322
What is the Eightfold Path?

102
00:09:59,361 --> 00:10:00,744
We know that off by heart, of course.

103
00:10:01,543 --> 00:10:09,211
And the three aggregates are now, here again, the use of the word, the three aggregates included in the Noble Eightfold Path.

104
00:10:09,231 --> 00:10:15,157
This is the way that the Noble Eightfold Path is split, sila, samadhi, and panya.

105
00:10:15,216 --> 00:10:21,523
So sila is our ethical conduct, samadhi, our meditation, and panya, our wisdom.

106
00:10:21,857 --> 00:10:37,376
And so the way it's put here is right effort, three aggregates are not included in the Noble Eightfold Path, but the Noble Eightfold Path is included in the three aggregates.

107
00:10:37,596 --> 00:10:50,633
So in other words, the Noble Eightfold Path is made up of these eight factors, three, I mean, it translates as aggregate, what would you say, three sections.

108
00:10:51,472 --> 00:11:15,017
right speech right action and right livelihood these are the aggregates of virtue okay ethics right effort right mindfulness and right concentration are the aggregates of concentration or wider still it's meditation yeah and right view and right intention these states are including the aggregate of wisdom okay

109
00:11:18,575 --> 00:11:23,423
Even though we say right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration is the aggregative concentration.

110
00:11:24,947 --> 00:11:32,041
If you think of that also in daily life, you know, just washing the pots, being aware, putting your attention there.

111
00:11:32,621 --> 00:11:34,144
So it's not just the meditation.

112
00:11:36,870 --> 00:11:37,071
Yeah.

113
00:11:37,091 --> 00:11:37,191
Okay.

114
00:11:39,263 --> 00:11:41,164
So then we can move on a little.

115
00:11:41,424 --> 00:11:42,846
So concentration now.

116
00:11:42,907 --> 00:11:44,128
See, what is concentration?

117
00:11:44,168 --> 00:11:46,610
